  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 358 AM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nCurrent upper-level water vapor imagery showing troughing over New \nBrunswick, Canada departing northeast, while broad troughing \nenters the PacNorthwest. Saddled in between these two features \nis a riding pattern that extends from the Intermountain-West \nover to the Great Lakes region. Below this ridging pattern, a \nshortwave was located over the MN and Dakotas border that will \nprogress to the east southeast today. Mid-level warm advection \ncombined with an intensifying 40-50kt low level jet will aid in \nadditional moisture transport into the northern plains. This \nhas resulted in rainfall becoming more widespread since 1 AM \nthis morning across much of western Minnesota and will continue \neast over the next several hours. A few rumbles of thunder could\nbe common, especially across western MN where an axis of mixed \nlayer instability between 500-1000 J/k exists. Much of this \nrainfall should be captured during the first half of today. \nAlthough most areas can expect the rainfall to wrap up by 10 AM \nthis morning, western Wisconsin will lag closer to noon before \nmost rain has passed on. Most areas have a favorable shot at \npicking up 0.25\" of rainfall with localized areas receiving \ncloser to 0.75\". Once the rain clears, sky coverage will vary \ndepending on location across MN/WI today. For those south and \nwest of I-94 have a stronger shot at seeing partly cloudy skies \nwhereas elsewhere can expect broken sunshine by this afternoon. \nHigh temperatures this afternoon will also reflect where \nsunshine is more prevalent, i.e southwest MN reaching the mid to\nupper 80s, eastern MN near 80, western WI, mid to low 70s. \n\nAfter today, the weather pattern becomes more consistent with zonal \nflow aloft, warmer temperatures, and daily rainfall chances. On \nSunday, another subtle wave moves across central MN Sunday morning \nfeaturing a broad surface low that will bring another chance for \nshowers and potentially a strong storm Sunday afternoon. Latest \nforecast guidance favor more of central MN track this time but given \nsome inconsistencies on storm coverage, was reluctant to go \nastray from current NBM PoPs. Key takeaway here is that most \nareas are better suited to remain dry versus seeing rain. As for\ntemps, Sunday and Monday will feel the most humid with dew \npoints in the low 70s and highs in the upper-80s across southern\nMN. As the week continues, dew points will improve slightly but\nhighs will stay in the 80s with lows in 60s.",
